CAT = 'sgm9Traditional'; SEG = 'TRD'
Q = [
('A plan that pays only if death or TPD occurs within a set period, and usually has no cash value, is:',
 'Term insurance', ['Whole life insurance', 'An endowment', 'Universal life'],
 'Term cover protects for a set period only. With no savings element it is usually cheaper and has no cash value if cancelled.'),
('A plan that pays the sum assured plus bonuses at the maturity date, or earlier on death, is:',
 'An endowment', ['Term insurance', 'A life annuity', 'A pure term rider'],
 'An endowment combines protection and savings: it pays on death or TPD within the term, or at maturity if the insured survives.'),
('Whole life cover where premiums stop after 20 years but cover continues for life is:',
 'Limited payment whole life', ['Decreasing term assurance', 'Twenty-year renewable term', 'Twenty-year pure endowment'],
 'Limited payment whole life compresses the premiums into a shorter period, while the life cover still runs for the whole of life.'),
('A term plan whose sum assured reduces in step with an outstanding home loan is:',
 'Mortgage reducing term', ['Level term assurance', 'Convertible term policy', 'Increasing term assurance'],
 'Mortgage reducing term assurance falls roughly in line with the loan balance, so it pays off the remaining debt if the borrower dies.'),
('A renewable term policy lets the insured, at the end of each term:',
 'Renew without new health evidence', ['Renew at the original entry-age premium', 'Switch into an ILP for free', 'Receive the premiums back'],
 'Renewability lets the policy continue without fresh underwriting, but the new premium is based on the age reached at renewal.'),
('A conversion option on a term policy lets the insured:',
 'Switch to permanent cover, no underwriting', ['Change the life assured at any time', 'Cash in the term policy for its full value', 'Pay premiums in another currency'],
 'Convertible term lets the insured exchange the term policy for whole life or endowment cover without new health evidence, within the dates set in the policy.'),
('Direct Purchase Insurance (DPI) products can be bought:',
 'Without advice or commission', ['Only through tied agents', 'Only with CPF savings', 'Only as ILPs'],
 'DPI products are sold directly by insurers without financial advice, so no commission is paid and premiums are lower than for comparable advised products.'),
('DPI products in Singapore can be recognised by which word at the start of their names?',
 'DIRECT', ['SIMPLE', 'BASIC', 'ESSENTIAL'],
 'LIA explains that DPI products carry the prefix "DIRECT" in their product name and are offered by every life insurer serving retail customers.'),
('Which two kinds of plan are offered as Direct Purchase Insurance?',
 'Term and whole life', ['Endowments and annuities', 'ILPs and universal life', 'Hospital and travel plans'],
 'DPI is a class of simple term and whole life products with TPD cover and an optional critical illness rider, with broadly standard features.'),
('The cash value of a universal life policy usually grows by:',
 'Interest at a declared rate', ['Bonuses declared from the par fund', 'Changes in sub-fund unit prices', 'A fixed 10% each year'],
 'Universal life is interest sensitive: the cash value earns a declared crediting rate that can change, and most plans guarantee a minimum rate.'),
('Compared with traditional whole life, a key feature of universal life is:',
 'Flexible premiums', ['No death benefit', 'No cash value', 'Units bought at NAV'],
 'Within set limits, a universal life policyholder chooses how much to pay and when, so the policy should be reviewed regularly.'),
('compareFIRST, run with MAS and LIA, lets consumers:',
 'Compare life products across insurers', ['Buy policies at a discount', 'Lodge complaints with insurers', "See each agent's commission"],
 'compareFIRST is an information portal for comparing DPI, term, whole life and endowment products. It does not sell or promote policies.'),
('A young parent needs a large amount of cover at the lowest premium. Which plan usually fits best?',
 'Level term', ['Single premium endowment', 'Limited pay whole life', 'Pure endowment'],
 'Term cover has no savings element, so it gives the most death cover per dollar of premium for a set period.'),
('A pure endowment pays out:',
 'Only if the insured survives', ['Only on death in the term', 'On death or survival', 'Only on total disability'],
 'A pure endowment is a survival benefit only. An endowment assurance adds a death benefit within the term.'),
('A joint-life first-death policy covering a couple pays when:',
 'Either of them dies first', ['The second of the two lives dies', 'Both die in one accident', 'Either one retires'],
 'Joint-life first-death cover pays on the first death and then ends. Second-death cover waits until both lives have died.'),
('A regular premium whole life policy usually starts to build a cash value after about:',
 'Three years', ['Three months', 'Ten years', 'One year'],
 'Early premiums mostly meet set-up costs and cover, so regular premium whole life and endowment plans usually acquire a cash value after about three years.'),
('A single premium endowment usually has a cash value:',
 'Straight away', ['After three years', 'Only at maturity', 'Only after a claim'],
 'Single premium whole life and endowment policies usually have a cash value at once, although cashing in early may carry a charge.'),
('A non-participating whole life policy pays:',
 'Only its guaranteed benefits', ['Its benefits plus bonuses', 'Unit value plus bonuses', 'Premiums back with interest'],
 "A non-participating policy does not share in the life fund's profits, so no bonuses are added; only the stated benefits are paid."),
('An increasing term plan mainly helps the cover keep pace with:',
 'Inflation', ['Mortality', 'Policy loans', 'Unit prices'],
 'Increasing term raises the sum assured at set intervals so that the real value of the cover is not eroded by rising prices.'),
('A family income benefit policy pays, on death within the term:',
 'An income until the term ends', ['A lump sum at maturity', 'A refund of premiums', 'Yearly reversionary bonuses'],
 'Family income benefit is a term plan that replaces income: it pays a regular amount from the death until the end of the chosen term.'),
('A policy whose benefits depend on the prices of units in chosen funds is classified as:',
 'Investment-linked', ['Participating', 'Non-participating', 'Universal life'],
 'Products are grouped by how benefits are set. In an investment-linked policy, values follow the unit prices of the sub-funds chosen.'),
('An employer buys group term life cover for its staff. The policy owner is:',
 'The employer', ['Each employee', 'The insurer', 'The staff union'],
 'In group insurance the organisation holds the contract and the staff are insured lives, which is why the nomination rules for individual policies do not apply.'),
('Which plan is meant to give lifelong protection with premiums usually paid for life?',
 'Ordinary whole life', ['Decreasing term', 'Ten-year endowment plan', 'Pure endowment'],
 'Ordinary whole life pays on death whenever it happens, with premiums payable throughout life unless the plan limits the payment term.'),
('Which feature generally makes term cover cheaper than an endowment for the same sum assured?',
 'No savings element', ['A shorter claims process', 'No medical underwriting', 'Lower agent licensing'],
 'An endowment premium must fund a maturity payment as well as cover. Term premiums pay only for the risk of death in the period.'),
]
